```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  actionLabel: string;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({
  children,
  actionLabel,
  onActivate,
}: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article
      className={[
        "@container/card",
        "w-full max-w-screen-lg",
        "rounded-lg border border-ink/20",
        "bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm",
      ].join(" ")}
    >
      <div
        className={[
          "grid min-w-0 grid-cols-1 gap-6",
          "@md/card:grid-cols-[minmax(0,3fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]",
        ].join(" ")}
      >
        <div className="min-w-0 text-base">{children}</div>
        <aside className="text-sm text-ink/70">sidebar</aside>
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        className={[
          "mt-6 rounded-md bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface",
          "focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2",
          "focus-visible:outline-brand",
        ].join(" ")}
        onClick={onActivate}
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the non-semantic `<div>` and exposes a real `<button>` with a visible accessible name. Native keyboard activation and focus behavior come for free. Keeping the button separate also avoids wrapping arbitrary `children`—which might themselves be interactive—inside a button. If activation navigates somewhere, use `<a href>` instead.

- `design_token_escape`: replaces inline hex, RGB, shadow, spacing, and typography values with the existing `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` tokens from [tailwind.config.ts](/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-andrew-dev-crimes/ad0f3f9a-0962-407f-b4e5-9d994a4a18e5/scratchpad/wt-eval/evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend/tailwind.config.ts). Derived muted/border treatments remain rooted in `ink`. If exact border or shadow styling matters, add semantic tokens centrally instead of reintroducing literals here.

- `responsive_fragility`: removes the `800px`, `720px`, `240px`, and fixed-height layout. The card becomes `width: 100%` with a tokenized maximum width, typography uses the type scale, and the content/sidebar grid changes from one to two fluid columns based on the card’s container rather than the viewport.

Because context found no tests, I’d add a focused component test covering button role/name, Tab focus, and Enter/Space activation, plus a browser layout check at narrow and wide container widths. No files were changed.
